hankelMatrix -- matrix with constant anti-diagonal entries
**********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        m = hankelMatrix(r,p,q)
        m = hankelMatrix(R,x,p,q)
        m = hankelMatrix(R,p,q)
        m = hankelMatrix(p,q)
  * Inputs:
      * r, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, matrix whose first row will
        be the entries of m
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, in this case, hankelMatrix will
        use the variables of R
      * x, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, if present,
        hankelMatrix will use the variables of R starting with x
      * p, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, number of rows
      * q, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, number of columns

Description
===========

A Hankel matrix (or catalecticant matrix) is a matrix with a repeated element
on each anti-diagonal, that is m_(i,j) depends only on i+j. If the matrix r is
given, then we set m_(i,j) = r_(0,i+j) if i+j<numcols r, and 0 otherwise. The
degrees of the rows of m are set to 0, and the degrees of the columns are set
to the degree of r_(0,0); thus the Hankel matrix is homogeneous iff all the
entries of the first row of r have the same degree.

If no ring or matrix is given, hankelMatrix defines a new ring S with p+q-1
variables X_i, and then calls hankelMatrix(vars S, p,q).

+-------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : p = 2;q=3;                                  |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : S = ZZ/101[x_0..x_(p+q-2)]                  |
|                                                 |
|o3 = S                                           |
|                                                 |
|o3 : PolynomialRing                              |
+-------------------------------------------------+
